{
  "$comment": "Which edition each territory uses, by language: one row per (territory, language[, church, rite]) and period. DRAFT, from the 2026 Roman Missal survey. `territory` is ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 (XK for Kosovo) or ISO 3166-2 where a country has several authorities; a lookup falls back from a subdivision to its country. `church` (CESIDR esi:) defaults to esi:latin and `rite` (data/rites.json) to rite:roman. `status`: in_force (the edition issued or formally adopted for the territory), de_facto (a shared edition used without the territory's own edition or a recorded adoption), adopting (approved, pending local promulgation), unknown (no edition recorded: `edition` is null). `language` is BCP 47, compared case-insensitively; `und` marks a row for a set of languages the survey doesn't name individually. `from`/`until` bound an in_force period where known; a missing `from` means the edition's own in_force date, or else its publication year — never earlier. The edition of a book in force on a date is the in_force row for that book whose period contains it, with church esi:latin and rite rite:roman unless asked otherwise. Without an in_force row, the de_facto row applies, under the same lower bound: a date before its edition was published resolves to nothing. Rows cover the Missal for now; other books keep the edition-level scope rule until they get rows.",
